1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Wikipedia contributors. "<em>[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Meade,_3rd_Earl_of_Clanwilliam Richard Meade, 3rd Earl of Clanwilliam]</em>." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia. 
2 CONT * Burke, Bernard, Sir. [http://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhera00inburk#page/228/mode/2up A genealogical and heraldic dictionary of the peerage and baronetage of the British Empire]. London : Harrison 1869. page 229
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Richard Charles Francis Christian Meade, 3rd Earl of Clanwilliam GCH, styled Lord Gillford between 1800 and 1805, was a British diplomat and politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meade was the only son of Richard Meade, 2nd Earl of Clanwilliam, and Caroline, Countess Thun and succeeded in the earldom at the age of ten. His early years were spent in Vienna, where his father had moved after a series of bitte
2 CONC r quarrels with his own parents about his marriage and about their enormous debts, which deprived him of what should have been a great inheritance. After his father's death Richard was raised by relatives in England. He was educat
2 CONC ed at Eton.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his 1848 memoirs, François-René de Chateaubriand writes of Meade that "at the head of the younger [London dandies of the 1820s] . . . Lord Clanwilliam was prominent, the son, it was said, of the Duc de Richelieu. He did wonderf
2 CONC ul things: he rode his horse to Richmond, and returned to Almack's having fallen off twice. He had a certain trick of speaking in the manner of Alcibiades, which delighted."
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Clanwilliam joined the Diplomatic Service. He attended Lord Castlereagh's suite at the Congress of Vienna in 1814 and was his private secretary from 1817 to 1819 in the latter's capacity as Foreign Secretary. He was one of th
2 CONC e first people to see Castlereagh's widow after his suicide: she embraced him warmly, saying that Castlereagh had loved him. It was he who was largely responsible for the decision to give Lord Castlereagh an official funeral in We
2 CONC stminster Abbey, for which he was criticized by those who believed that Castlereagh's own wish was for a private family funeral. He was one of many witnesses who later testified to Castlereagh's increasingly strange mental conditi
2 CONC on in the days before his suicide.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became Under-Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs in 1822 and afterwards Envoy to Berlin from 1823 to 1827. In 1828, he was created Baron Clanwilliam, of Clanwilliam in the County of Tipperary, in the Peerage of the United Ki
2 CONC ngdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1847 he was awarded the honorary position of Captain of Deal Castle, which he held until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Clanwilliam married Lady Elizabeth Herbert, daughter of George Herbert, 11th Earl of Pembroke, on 5 July 1830. He died in 1879 and was succeeded by his eldest son, Richard. His second son, Sir Robert Henry Meade, later achiev
2 CONC ed distinction as Permanent Under-Secretary of the Colonial Office.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/274954853/richard_charles_francis_christian-meade
